# LoreKeeper — no draft access while debugging

When fixing or planning LoreKeeper (`lorekeeper/`, Ask, recall, save, deploy):

## Hard default — refuse unless owner says **override**

If the owner asks for anything below (even casually), **do not run it**. Explain in plain English why, and offer a code-only or synthetic-test path instead.

**Only bypass this rule when the owner deliberately types `override` in the same message** (e.g. “override — browser test Ask on my live notes”). Without that exact word, treat the request as **not approved**.

## Do not read the owner's story data

- **Never** SSH, `scp`, `curl`, or run server commands to open, copy, grep, or dump user note/document content (e.g. `lorekeeper-store.json`, `lorekeeper-data/`, `/api/user/data`, account blobs).
- **Never** ask the owner to paste draft text, scenes, or note bodies to debug.
- **Do** fix from repo code and **synthetic test text only** (fake names like Marcus/Kael — not the owner's real canon).

Deploy scripts and `nginx` reload are fine; they do **not** require reading note bodies.

## No browser tests on real drafts

- **Do not** use browser automation on `oddtrove.art/lorekeeper/` (or any live LoreKeeper URL) to inspect Ask answers, notes, or documents.
- Default: local reasoning and synthetic Python checks only.
- With **`override`** only: owner accepts visibility risk; prefer throwaway test notes or signed-out session; do not quote real draft text back in chat.

## Chat hygiene

- Describe fixes in plain English — do not echo the owner's story from any source.
- If unsure whether an action crosses this line, **stop and ask** — do not guess.
